"0\" border=\"0\" bgcolor=\"#2c2c2e\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:600px;border-collapse:collapse;background-color:#2c2c2e;margin:0 auto;text-align:left\"><tr><td class=\"hd-body\" bgcolor=\"#2c2c2e\" style=\"padding:6px 16px 20px;background-color:#2c2c2e;color:#d1d1d6;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;text-align:left;overflow-wrap:break-word;word-wrap:break-word\">\n<p class=\"hd-inkrule hd-ink\" style=\"margin:0;padding:6px 0 5px;border-top:3px double #f2f2f7;border-bottom:1px solid #f2f2f7;text-align:center;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Helvetica Neue',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:9px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:1.6px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#f2f2f7\">Spin-free news · Ranked by impact</p>\n<table role=\"presentation\" width=\"100%\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" border=\"0\" style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse\"><tr><td align=\"center\" valign=\"top\" style=\"padding:24px 0 0;text-align:center;line-height:0;font-size:0\"><table role=\"presentation\" class=\"hd-seal-t\" align=\"center\" width=\"140\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" border=\"0\" style=\"width:140px;margin:0 auto;border-collapse:collapse\"><tr><td align=\"center\" valign=\"top\" style=\"text-align:center;line-height:0;font-size:0;padding:0\"><a href=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/\" style=\"display:block;text-align:center;margin:0 auto;text-decoration:none;border:0\"><img class=\"hd-seal\" src=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/assets/email/seal.png\" alt=\"High Impact News Daily\" width=\"140\" height=\"140\" border=\"0\" style=\"display:block;margin:0 auto;width:140px;max-width:100%;height:auto;border:0;outline:none;text-decoration:none\"></a></td></tr></table></td></tr></table>\n<p style=\"margin:12px 0 0;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;line-height:1.1\"><a href=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/\" style=\"text-decoration:none\"><span class=\"hd-ink hd-wm-p\" style=\"font-family:'IM Fell English',Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:25px;letter-spacing:1px;color:#f2f2f7\">HIGH</span> <span class=\"hd-accent hd-wm-s\" style=\"font-family:'Pinyon Script','Snell Roundhand',cursive;font-size:31px;margin:0 3px;color:#e08a6f\">impact</span> <span class=\"hd-ink hd-wm-p\" style=\"font-family:'IM Fell English',Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:25px;letter-spacing:1px;color:#f2f2f7\">NEWS</span> <span class=\"hd-accent hd-wm-s\" style=\"font-family:'Pinyon Script','Snell Roundhand',cursive;font-size:31px;margin:0 3px;color:#e08a6f\">daily</span></a></p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0;text-align:center;line-height:0\"><img src=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/assets/email/orn-head.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"260\" height=\"20\" style=\"display:inline-block;width:260px;max-width:80%;height:auto;border:0\"></p>\n\n<p class=\"hd-accent\" style=\"margin:18px 0 0;text-align:center;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Helvetica Neue',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#e08a6f\">Evening edition · Saturday, October 31</p>\n<p class=\"hd-muted\" style=\"margin:4px 0 6px;text-align:center;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-style:italic;font-size:14px;color:#8e8e93\">What happened since this morning. Five minutes. No spin.</p>\n<h3 class=\"hd-ink\" style=\"margin:20px 0 8px;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:600;color:#f2f2f7\">Trump&#x27;s planned 250-foot &quot;Triumphal Arch&quot; faces a new lawsuit from Virginia and the District of Columbia, filed days before construction is set to begin.</h3>\n<p class=\"hd-body\" style=\"margin:0 0 10px;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;color:#d1d1d6\">Virginia Attorney General Jay Jones and D.C. Attorney General Brian Schwalb sued the National Park Service and the Interior Department in federal court in Washington on Saturday, asking for a temporary restraining order. They argue the arch at Memorial Circle, by Arlington Memorial Bridge and the entrance to Arlington National Cemetery, lacks the approval from Congress that the Commemorative Works Act requires, would break D.C.&#x27;s 130-foot height limit, and skipped environmental and historic-preservation reviews. They say three years of construction would snarl traffic between the two jurisdictions and pollute the Potomac. The administration has said work could start as early as Nov. 5. It is the third suit against the arch; U.S. District Judge Tanya Chutkan heard arguments Friday in the first, brought by Vietnam veterans and a historian, and said she would rule quickly.</p>\n<p class=\"hd-muted\" style=\"margin:0;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Helvetica Neue',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;line-height:1.45;color:#8e8e93\"><img src=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/assets/email/impact-3.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" style=\"width:50px;max-width:100%;height:auto;vertical-align:-3px;border:0\"> <b class=\"hd-ink\" style=\"color:#f2f2f7\">Impact 3/5.</b> Courts could decide within days whether the president can build a monument taller than the Statue of Liberty without Congress. <a class=\"hd-accent\" href=\"https://apnews.com/article/trump-memorial-arch-lawsuits-virginia-district-columbia-f71a43143f8c8734fbd9aa3ceebfd1d6\" style=\"color:#e08a6f;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600\">AP ↗</a></p>\n<p style=\"margin:22px 0 0;text-align:center;line-height:0\"><img src=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/assets/email/orn-sep.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"180\" height=\"18\" style=\"display:inline-block;width:180px;max-width:60%;height:auto;border:0\"></p>\n<h3 class=\"hd-ink\" style=\"margin:22px 0 8px;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:600;color:#f2f2f7\">Poland&#x27;s court crisis deepened Saturday as President Karol Nawrocki pushed into the Constitutional Tribunal overnight, and crisis talks ended with no plan.</h3>\n<p class=\"hd-body\" style=\"margin:0 0 10px;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;color:#d1d1d6\">Nawrocki, back from an aborted trip to Senegal, entered the tribunal around 1 a.m. Warsaw time and told judges backed by the current parliament, including Anna Korwin-Piotrowska, &quot;You are not judges of the Constitutional Tribunal&quot;; Korwin-Piotrowska said he barged into them, and each side blames the other for the scuffle. In a statement read outside the court, he called it a &quot;violation of the constitutional order,&quot; demanded police withdraw and called urgent talks citing &quot;the threat to internal security.&quot; Those talks with several parties produced no joint plan; Prime Minister Donald Tusk&#x27;s Civic Coalition and the Left stayed away. In an evening address, Tusk vowed &quot;no compromise&quot; on the rule of law and defended the newly elected judges. Police said three protesters were arrested Friday; the court&#x27;s own X account called the moves a &quot;coup,&quot; a word Law and Justice leaders also use.</p>\n<p class=\"hd-muted\" style=\"margin:0;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Helvetica Neue',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;line-height:1.45;color:#8e8e93\"><img src=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/assets/email/impact-3.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" style=\"width:50px;max-width:100%;height:auto;vertical-align:-3px;border:0\"> <b class=\"hd-ink\" style=\"color:#f2f2f7\">Impact 3/5.</b> The fight over a NATO ally&#x27;s top court is now a direct standoff between its president and government. <a class=\"hd-accent\" href=\"https://www.afp.com/en/polish-president-calls-crisis-talks-after-court-standoff\" style=\"color:#e08a6f;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600\">AFP ↗</a></p>\n<p style=\"margin:22px 0 0;text-align:center;line-height:0\"><img src=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/assets/email/orn-sep.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"180\" height=\"18\" style=\"display:inline-block;width:180px;max-width:60%;height:auto;border:0\"></p>\n<h3 class=\"hd-ink\" style=\"margin:22px 0 8px;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:600;color:#f2f2f7\">Iran&#x27;s supreme leader, Mojtaba Khamenei, ordered the armed forces to answer any threat immediately and &quot;without geographic limits,&quot; with U.S. and Israeli assets added to target lists.</h3>\n<p class=\"hd-body\" style=\"margin:0 0 10px;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;color:#d1d1d6\">Khamenei gave the order Saturday at a meeting with senior commanders, according to a statement on his website; he told them to update the military&#x27;s target list to include U.S. and Israeli assets. Separately, Iranian state TV, citing a provincial official in Hormozgan, said U.S. forces attacked two fishing boats near Qeshm Island, wounding one fisherman; two missing men were later found. The U.S. military has not confirmed the claim, and the White House and Pentagon did not immediately respond. Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi called Vice President JD Vance&#x27;s letter a &quot;positive step&quot; but said Iran has no plans to meet U.S. officials, as an Iranian team holds talks with Qatari and Pakistani mediators in Doha.</p>\n<p class=\"hd-muted\" style=\"margin:0;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Helvetica Neue',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;line-height:1.45;color:#8e8e93\"><img src=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/assets/email/impact-3.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" style=\"width:50px;max-width:100%;height:auto;vertical-align:-3px;border:0\"> <b class=\"hd-ink\" style=\"color:#f2f2f7\">Impact 3/5.</b> It hardens Iran&#x27;s stance just as mediators try to restart U.S.-Iran talks and head off renewed war. <a class=\"hd-accent\" href=\"https://apnews.com/article/mideast-news-october-31-2026-15c0556b92c8c23beb9b8854b4c124f4\" style=\"color:#e08a6f;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600\">AP ↗</a></p>\n<p style=\"margin:22px 0 0;text-align:center;line-height:0\"><img src=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/assets/email/orn-sep.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"180\" height=\"18\" style=\"display:inline-block;width:180px;max-width:60%;height:auto;border:0\"></p>\n<h3 class=\"hd-ink\" style=\"margin:22px 0 8px;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:600;color:#f2f2f7\">Early voting passed 52 million ballots heading into the final weekend before Tuesday&#x27;s midterms, a record for a midterm election.</h3>\n<p class=\"hd-body\" style=\"margin:0 0 10px;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;color:#d1d1d6\">The University of Florida Election Lab&#x27;s tracker, last updated 4:31 p.m. Saturday, shows about 52.3 million early votes: about 28.9 million cast in person and 23.5 million mail ballots returned, with more than 60 million mail ballots requested. Reuters reported the total had already set a midterm record. Some states do not update daily, so the count will keep rising before Election Day, Nov. 3.</p>\n<p class=\"hd-muted\" style=\"margin:0;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Helvetica Neue',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;line-height:1.45;color:#8e8e93\"><img src=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/assets/email/impact-2.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" style=\"width:50px;max-width:100%;height:auto;vertical-align:-3px;border:0\"> <b class=\"hd-ink\" style=\"color:#f2f2f7\">Impact 2/5.</b> Turnout is running at a pace not seen in a midterm. <a class=\"hd-accent\" href=\"https://election.lab.ufl.edu/early-vote/2026-early-voting/\" style=\"color:#e08a6f;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600\">University of Florida Election Lab ↗</a></p>\n<p style=\"margin:22px 0 0;text-align:center;line-height:0\"><img src=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/assets/email/orn-sep.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"180\" height=\"18\" style=\"display:inline-block;width:180px;max-width:60%;height:auto;border:0\"></p>\n<h3 class=\"hd-ink\" style=\"margin:22px 0 8px;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:600;color:#f2f2f7\">The G20 foreign ministers&#x27; meeting hosted by Secretary of State Marco Rubio in Atlanta ended without a joint statement, split over Russia&#x27;s war in Ukraine.</h3>\n<p class=\"hd-body\" style=\"margin:0 0 10px;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;color:#d1d1d6\">After two days of talks, Rubio released a chair&#x27;s statement listing priorities, including energy security, supply chains and critical minerals, and safe passage through the Strait of Hormuz and Bab al-Mandab, but no agreements. &quot;I wouldn&#x27;t pretend to you today that we are close to finding universally accepted solutions to these problems,&quot; Rubio told reporters. The meeting opened Friday amid European objections to Russian Foreign Minister Sergey Lavrov&#x27;s presence; EU foreign policy chief Kaja Kallas accused Lavrov of &quot;a lot of lies.&quot; The U.S. holds the G20 presidency this year.</p>\n<p class=\"hd-muted\" style=\"margin:0;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Helvetica Neue',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;line-height:1.45;color:#8e8e93\"><img src=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/assets/email/impact-2.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" style=\"width:50px;max-width:100%;height:auto;vertical-align:-3px;border:0\"> <b class=\"hd-ink\" style=\"color:#f2f2f7\">Impact 2/5.</b> The U.S.-hosted G20 could not agree on the biggest security issues of the year. <a class=\"hd-accent\" href=\"https://apnews.com/article/rubio-g20-ukraine-russia-poland-21c65a8bb02a3a0f0c89f0ce6d56d5fd\" style=\"color:#e08a6f;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600\">AP ↗</a></p>\n<p style=\"margin:22px 0 0;text-align:center;line-height:0\"><img src=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/assets/email/orn-sep.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"180\" height=\"18\" style=\"display:inline-block;width:180px;max-width:60%;height:auto;border:0\"></p>\n<h3 class=\"hd-ink\" style=\"margin:22px 0 8px;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:600;color:#f2f2f7\">A shooting inside a Spirit Halloween store at a Fairfield, Calif., mall killed one person and wounded six, and the gunman was still being sought Saturday.</h3>\n<p class=\"hd-body\" style=\"margin:0 0 10px;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;color:#d1d1d6\">Gunfire broke out about 8:30 p.m. Friday Pacific time at the Solano Town Center. Police said Saturday the shooting stemmed from a targeted incident involving one suspect and that detectives were reviewing video to identify the shooter; no one has been detained. One person died at the scene and three were critically injured. The FBI&#x27;s Sacramento office sent an evidence team, and the mall canceled its Halloween trick-or-treat event.</p>\n<p class=\"hd-muted\" style=\"margin:0;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Helvetica Neue',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;line-height:1.45;color:#8e8e93\"><img src=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/assets/email/impact-2.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" style=\"width:50px;max-width:100%;height:auto;vertical-align:-3px;border:0\"> <b class=\"hd-ink\" style=\"color:#f2f2f7\">Impact 2/5.</b> A mass shooting at a crowded holiday store has put a California city on edge on Halloween. <a class=\"hd-accent\" href=\"https://apnews.com/article/california-fairfield-shooting-solano-town-center-c531ed034832fa0558a246b18334f5bd\" style=\"color:#e08a6f;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600\">AP ↗</a></p>\n<p style=\"margin:22px 0 0;text-align:center;line-height:0\"><img src=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/assets/email/orn-sep.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"180\" height=\"18\" style=\"display:inline-block;width:180px;max-width:60%;height:auto;border:0\"></p>\n<h3 class=\"hd-ink\" style=\"margin:22px 0 8px;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:600;color:#f2f2f7\">Evacuees from Russian-occupied Oleshky reached Ukrainian-held territory Saturday under the local truce brokered by first lady Melania Trump.</h3>\n<p class=\"hd-body\" style=\"margin:0 0 10px;font-family:'Source Serif 4','Source Serif Pro',Georgia,'Times New Roman',Times,serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;color:#d1d1d6\">The convoy took a long route through Crimea and Russia into Belarus before crossing into Ukraine&#x27;s Chernihiv region; it had room for about 750 people. Officials have not given a final count of how many chose Ukraine rather than Russia. President Volodymyr Zelensky said the arrivals would get help and thanked the first lady. Fighting elsewhere went on: Zelensky said Russian strikes overnight left about 280,000 households without power.</p>\n<p class=\"hd-muted\" style=\"margin:0;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Helvetica Neue',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;line-height:1.45;color:#8e8e93\"><img src=\"https://hinewsdaily.com/assets/email/impact-2.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" style=\"width:50px;max-width:100%;height:auto;vertical-align:-3px;border:0\"> <b class=\"hd-ink\" style=\"color:#f2f2f7\">Impact 2/5.</b> Civilians trapped for years under occupation got out under a rare agreed pause in fighting, though it covers only one town. <a class=\"hd-accent\" href=\"https://apnews.co